Janis wrote:

> How is icebox defined? How is refrigerator defined? I could take my
> dictionary and see icebox translated as K?hlschrank and refrigerator
> as Eisschrank; though the words are used differently here depending on
> the province you live. I learned it as K?hlschrank (icebox) to operate
> on temperatures of typically +7 (3..10) degree Celsius and Eisschrank
> (refrigerator) down to -20 degree Celsius.

In Dutch, they would be translated as "koelkast" and "ijskast", and both 
would mean the same temperature (around 5 degrees Celcius). The 
difference between them is the way they function: a koelkast runs on 
electricity, while a ijskast runs on ijs (ice): you put a large lump if 
ice in it, which keeps it cold. Ice used to be sold in blocks in the 
streets when my grandparents were young, and is still sold that way for 
just this purpose in many countries.

What you call an Eisschrank above would be called a "vriezer" (freezer) 
in Dutch (English). As far as I know, a refrigerator is the same as an 
ice box in English...

